---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Home">
  <h1>The Lerena Archive</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An evidence-first archive of the <strong>Lerena</strong> family — carried, by family account,
    from <strong>Rosario</strong> in Argentina to <strong>South Africa</strong>, and from there to
    <strong>Brisbane</strong>. This archive begins almost empty, and says so.
  </p>

  <div class="card" style="margin:1.6rem 0">
    <p style="margin-top:0"><span class="chip doc">8 September 2026 — the line is documented</span></p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      The head of the family is <strong>Roberto Paul Lerena</strong>, born in <strong>South America</strong>
      about 1874, who died at Newlands, Cape Town on <strong>19 March 1950</strong>. The state recorded his
      occupation as <strong>Race Horse Owner</strong>. From him the descent runs unbroken and documented at
      every step to David Defranceski's mother. <a href={u("/estate")}>The estate &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What the family remembered, and how it has held up</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:14rem">The claim</th><th>Where it stands</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>South American origin</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Confirmed</span> His death certificate gives his birthplace as
            <strong>South America</strong>. An Argentine passport in the same family papers, for
            <strong>Pablo Armando Lerena</strong>, gives <strong>Rosario de Santa Fe, 22 February
            1882</strong> — though how that man relates to this line is
            <a href={u("/passport")}>not yet settled</a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>He brought horses</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Confirmed</span> <strong>Race Horse Owner</strong> on his death
            certificate; a 1931 municipal file for <strong>stables</strong> at Koeberg Road, Rugby; and,
            among the debts of his estate, a claim for <strong>&pound;26 of carrots</strong>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>He was Consul General</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip dis">Disputed</span> Nothing supports it. On the 1940 Argentine passport the
            consular signature belongs to A. B. Bayne, not to a Lerena.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>He had an ostrich farm</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip lore">Family lore</span> Untested. Nothing found for or against.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small">
    Recorded from David Defranceski on 8 September 2026 and tested the same day against the South African
    National Archives, the family's papers, and a Cape cemetery register.
    <a href={u("/direct-line")}>The line &rarr;</a>
  </p>

  <h2>What is already established</h2>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="card">
      <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span></p>
      <h3 style="margin-top:.4rem">The name is rare</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        <strong>2,124 bearers worldwide</strong> — the 197,177th commonest surname on earth, one person
        in 3.43 million. Compare <em>Falco</em>, with 46,749. Rarity is the single most useful fact in this
        archive: with a name this scarce, two clusters of Lerenas are far more likely to be one family
        than to be coincidence. <a href={u("/name")}>The name &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span></p>
      <h3 style="margin-top:.4rem">The River Plate is the heartland</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Spain has the most Lerenas in absolute terms (888), but <strong>Uruguay</strong> has them at
        <strong>1 in 7,962</strong> — over six times Spain's density and the highest on earth. Argentina
        holds 293. The name's centre of gravity is the River Plate, exactly where the family story starts.
        <a href={u("/argentina")}>Argentina &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span></p>
      <h3 style="margin-top:.4rem">There are Lerenas in the stud book</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        <strong>Gilberto Justiniano Lerena Lenguas</strong> co-founded the <strong>Argentine Stud
        Book</strong> and bred a Quadruple Crown winner; a Group race still carries his name. In South
        Africa, <strong>Gavin Lerena</strong> is a leading jockey. The horse thread is real on both
        continents — what is unproven is whether it runs through this family.
        <a href={u("/horses")}>The horses &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span></p>
      <h3 style="margin-top:.4rem">The South African branch is a founder cluster</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        <strong>61 Lerenas in South Africa</strong>. For a surname of 2,124 people worldwide that is a
        striking concentration, and it has the signature of a single family arriving and multiplying —
        which is precisely what the family story describes.
        <a href={u("/south-africa")}>South Africa &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The two origins problem</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Lerena</em> may be two different names wearing one spelling, and the archive cannot yet say which
    one belongs to this family:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Basque.</strong> The ending <em>-ena</em> is the Basque possessive — "the house of". This
      would make Lerena a house-name, and the family Basque or Navarrese.</li>
    <li><strong>Castilian toponymic.</strong> <strong>Llerena</strong> in Badajoz, Extremadura, is a real
      town with 36,828 name-bearers descended from it. <em>Ll-</em> reduces to <em>L-</em> readily in
      Spanish-American registers and at ports of arrival.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is the same problem the Falco archive met as <em>Falco</em> versus <em>De Falco</em>, and it is
    settled the same way: in the registers, not by the look of the name.
    <a href={u("/name")}>The full baseline &rarr;</a>
  </p>

  <h2>How this archive works</h2>
  <p>
    Every statement is tiered — <strong>documented</strong>, <strong>inferred</strong>,
    <strong>disputed</strong>, or <strong>family lore</strong> — and the tier is shown. Family lore is kept
    because it is precious and labelled because pretending otherwise is how family myths become family
    history. <strong>Living people are omitted from the build entirely.</strong>
    <a href={u("/method")}>Method &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</Base>
